C = [
("h1", "6. 1 Corinthians 8: Knowledge, Love and the Weak Conscience"),
("p", "Claim: 1 Corinthians 8 answers a different case from Romans 14. The Corinthians are eating meat offered to idols, some of them in the idol’s temple, and some of their brethren are being emboldened to eat against their conscience. Paul grants the knowledge of the strong, sets love above it, and gives himself as the example of the limit."),
("sub", "6.1 The case in Corinth"),
("p", "The chapter begins with a subject, not a person: “Now as touching things offered unto idols” (1 Corinthians 8:1). The case is meat that had been offered to an idol, which was afterwards eaten at the idol’s temple or sold in the market. The believers at Corinth had knowledge about it. Paul says, “we know that we all have knowledge” (1 Corinthians 8:1), and then he states what the knowledge is: “we know that an idol is nothing in the world, and that there is none other God but one” (1 Corinthians 8:4). He adds the Christian confession in full: “But to us there is but one God, the Father, of whom are all things, and we in him; and one Lord Jesus Christ, by whom are all things, and we by him” (1 Corinthians 8:6). The volume notes that Paul grants the knowledge. He does not say the strong are wrong about the idol, and he does not hedge what he says of the one God and one Lord."),
("sub", "6.2 Knowledge and charity"),
("p", "The ground of the limit comes before the case is described. “Knowledge puffeth up, but charity edifieth” (1 Corinthians 8:1). “And if any man think that he knoweth any thing, he knoweth nothing yet as he ought to know. But if any man love God, the same is known of him” (1 Corinthians 8:2, 3). Constable puts the point in a sentence: “Paul wrote that the real aim of the faith should not be knowledge but love.” The volume agrees, and it adds that the sentence is a ground for the limit and not a rebuke of knowledge. Paul does not tell the Corinthians to know less. He tells them that what they know does not decide what they should do."),
("sub", "6.3 The weak conscience in Corinth"),
("p", "“Howbeit there is not in every man that knowledge: for some with conscience of the idol unto this hour eat it as a thing offered unto an idol; and their conscience being weak is defiled” (1 Corinthians 8:7). The verse describes a weak man different from the weak man of Romans 14. In Rome the weak man abstains and the strong man eats. In Corinth the weak man eats, and his conscience is defiled by eating, because he eats “as a thing offered unto an idol.” He has not left the idol behind. The two cases are not the same, and the volume does not read the Corinthian case into the Roman one or the Roman into the Corinthian."),
("p", "“But meat commendeth us not to God: for neither, if we eat, are we the better; neither, if we eat not, are we the worse” (1 Corinthians 8:8). The verse says the same of meat that Romans 14:14 says: it has no standing before God either way. Constable draws the same line: “Foods do not make us more or less pleasing to God.”"),
("sub", "6.4 The liberty that becomes a stumblingblock"),
("p", "“But take heed lest by any means this liberty of yours become a stumblingblock to them that are weak” (1 Corinthians 8:9). Paul calls the strong man’s freedom “this liberty of yours.” It is a real liberty, and it can become a stumblingblock. The case he puts is specific: “For if any man see thee which hast knowledge sit at meat in the idol’s temple, shall not the conscience of him which is weak be emboldened to eat those things which are offered to idols;” (1 Corinthians 8:10). The weak man sees the strong man sitting at table in the idol’s temple, and he is emboldened to eat. He eats with his conscience still against it. The harm is not the strong man’s eating. The harm is the weak man’s eating on the strength of the example."),
("p", "“And through thy knowledge shall the weak brother perish, for whom Christ died?” (1 Corinthians 8:11). “But when ye sin so against the brethren, and wound their weak conscience, ye sin against Christ” (1 Corinthians 8:12). Two things are said in these verses and the volume reports both. The weak brother may “perish,” and those who wound his conscience “sin against Christ.” Paul does not say what the perishing is. He says that Christ died for the brother, and that to wound a brother is to sin against Christ. The ground of the limit is Christ’s death for the brother, as in Romans 14:15."),
("sub", "6.5 Paul’s own resolution"),
("p", "“Wherefore, if meat make my brother to offend, I will eat no flesh while the world standeth, lest I make my brother to offend” (1 Corinthians 8:13). Paul does not state a rule for the Corinthians. He states what he himself will do. The resolution is conditional: “if meat make my brother to offend.” It is also extreme: he will eat no flesh for as long as the world stands. The volume reads the verse as an example and an offer, not a command to the church. The command is in verse 9, “take heed.” The example in verse 13 shows how far Paul will go for a brother, and chapter 7 of this volume reads how he describes the same readiness in 1 Corinthians 9."),
("ctbl", "1 Corinthians 8 by the five questions.", [
    ("Question", "Answer from the passage", "Verse"),
    ("Does Paul command?", "He commands care: take heed; he does not forbid the eating of meat", "8:9"),
    ("What limit?", "Do not let your liberty become a stumblingblock to the weak", "8:9"),
    ("To whom is it owed?", "To the weak brother, for whom Christ died", "8:11, 12"),
    ("What ground?", "Charity edifieth; meat commendeth us not to God; Christ died for him", "8:1, 8, 11"),
    ("What is unsaid?", "What the perishing is; how many were weak; whether the strong should sit in the temple at all", "Chapters 6 and 16"),
  ], [0.26, 0.52, 0.22]),
("sub", "6.6 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "Paul grants the strong their knowledge and sets love above it as the ground of the limit.",
    "The weak man of Corinth eats and is defiled by it; the weak man of Rome abstains. The cases differ.",
    "The liberty of the strong becomes a stumblingblock when it emboldens the weak to eat against conscience.",
    "Paul gives his own resolution as an example and does not make it the church’s rule.",
  ]),
]
